283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (We have all sat through them. Four experts, polished talking points, and a rushed Q&A. Panel discussions are the default format for professional events, but they rarely deliver actual value.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (The problem is structural. Panellists prepare, and audiences consume passively, so there is no real knowledge exchange. It is just broadcasting. Moderators avoid controversy, and everyone leaves with the same knowledge they came with.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Research confirms the issue. Attention drops significantly after just ten minutes of one-way communication. Passive listening simply does not drive engagement or meaningful learning.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (For our upcoming event on breaking into data roles in 2026, we are flipping the script. What starts as a panel quickly breaks into interest-based groups where students and professionals debate strategies, question assumptions, and share real experiments. We are designing for value, rather than just content, by discussing observed trends, identifying potential failures, and testing ideas together.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (The logic is simple. Seed ideas broadly, then work on them deeply through dialogue. Real learning happens when students challenge professionals and peers debate approaches)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
